            "createdOn": 1782261004,
            "author": {
                "name": "Elton Wang",
                "email": "elton.wang.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                "username": "EltonWang-wiwynn"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Do we actually need an entirely different platform/image for this?  Isn't it close enough to the Yosemite5 platform that we can reuse it?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jagpal S Gill",
                        "email": "paligill@gmail.com",
                        "username": "jagpalgill"
                    },
                    "message": "I don't see any difference (from actual yv5) in the platform config files being introduced as part of this patch, not sure what extra will get added later, probably @elton.wang.wiwynn@gmail.com can provide details on that.\n\nBut yes, if there is going to be not much difference overall then it makes sense to have same image."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Elton Wang",
                        "email": "elton.wang.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                        "username": "EltonWang-wiwynn"
                    },
                    "message": "Bigbend (Yosemite5 T22) introduces a new SSD expansion board (Rio Grande) and a new 3kW paddle board, replacing the 1kW paddle board used on Yosemite5.\nThese boards add and remove I2C devices and extend the bus with new muxes, giving Bigbend a different I2C topology from Yosemite5 that requires its own device tree and EM configurations.\nTherefore, a new platform layer is needed to hold these platform-specific configurations (e.g. system early initialization, EM blocklist, etc.)."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jerry C Chen",
                        "email": "jerry.c.chen.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                        "username": "Jerry-wiwynn"
                    },
                    "message": "Hi @patrick@stwcx.xyz @paligill@gmail.com \nThe main difference in the T22 project is that it includes a new SSD expansion board and an additional NIC, which requires a new DTS file to support the new virtual bus and the new I2C topology (different frequency and configuration).\nIf you want to use the same image for both projects, we may need to load the DTB file dynamically. Please let us know if you have any concerns or suggestions regarding this. Thanks."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Amithash Prasad",
                        "email": "amithash@meta.com",
                        "username": "amithash"
                    },
                    "message": "Whats the problem with having the DTS contain entries for the new SSD expansion and NIC within the existing DTS? Wont the kernel ignore these entires when the device does not exist?\n\nIMHO the only thing requiring dynamic-dtb/new-dts is if the two platform types had completely different device types on the same bus/addr. But having additional devices which are present in one config but absent in another should be totally viable.\n\nWe could have the design team for Y5 and other derivatives mark these bus/addr combos as reserved and ensure that they are never used for different future variants.\n\nEntitiy Manager design in other regards should totally support these type of variants without needing a totally different DTS."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jagpal S Gill",
                        "email": "paligill@gmail.com",
                        "username": "jagpalgill"
                    },
                    "message": "@jerry.c.chen.wiwynn@gmail.com Since these are new entries, we suppose you can add the new entries into the existing Yv5 DTS and they should get ignored on the base Yv5 when kernel boot (as physically nothing would be connected there). And since BigBend will have its own EM config, it will always enumerate right set of configs for it.\n(CC @patrick@stwcx.xyz @amithash@meta.com )"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "There really isn't such a thing as \"loading a DTB file dynamically\".\n\ndbus-sensors and fru-device will typically probe devices that aren't in the DTS but are specified in EM configs.  Can all of the storage expander devices be represented in the EM config for that card and then dynamically probed?"
                }
            ],
